<project>
    <title>Cryptanalysis of substitution cipher</title>
<p>
    Eve has intercepted the following ciphertext message, and suspects it was encrypted using a substitution cipher. 
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
        c=''
    </input>
    <output>
    
    </output>
</sage>
<p>
    Here is function that performs frequency analysis on a string.
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
def freq_anal(s):
    s=list(s)
    table={x:s.count(x) for x in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz'}
    bigrams={}
    for i in range(len(s)-1):
        di=s[i]+s[i+1]
        bigrams[di]=bigrams.get(di,0)+1
    return table, bigrams
    </input>
    <output>
        
    </output>
</sage>
<p>
    Let's look at the top ten most common letters and bigrams. 
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
l,b=freq_anal(c)
for x in sorted(l.keys(),key=lambda k:l[k], reverse=True)[:10]:
    print(x, l[x]/len(c))
for x in sorted(b.keys(),key=lambda k:b[k], reverse=True)[:50]:
    print(x, b[x])
    </input>
    <output>
        
    </output>
</sage>
<p>
    The the next function allows us to make guesses about what the letter substitutions are and update our guess at the deciphered text. 
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
        t=['_']*len(c)
guesses={}
def guess(x,y):
    guesses[x]=y
    for i in range(len(c)):
        if c[i]==x: t[i]=y
    print('Guesses: ', guesses)
    k=50
    for i in range(len(c)//50):
        print('\n'+c[i*k:(i+1)*k]+'\n')
        tstr=str()
        for x in t[i*k: (i+1)*k]: tstr=tstr+x
        print(tstr)
    </input>
    <output>
        
    </output>
</sage>
<p>
    Use the Sage cell below to decrypt the intercepted message. If you want to start your guesses from scratch, run the cell above this paragraph again. 
</p>
<sage>
    <input>
        
    </input>
    <output>
        
    </output>
</sage>
    
</project>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ss_vignere_cipher">
    <title>Vigenère cipher</title>
    <p>
        What made the substitution cipher vulnerable to frequency analysis is that the encryption was defined on the level of single letters. We call this a <em>monographic</em> cryptosystem. A <em>polygraphic</em> (<em>block</em>) cryptosystem, in contrast, defines an encrpytion method on a block, or list of letters. The <em>Vignère cipher</em> defined below is a classic example of a block cipher that at first glance appears to be invulnerable to frequency analysis, and indeed was long considered to be unbreakable. 
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="d_vignere">
    <title>Vigenère cipher</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
    Let <m>\mathcal{A}=\{0,1,\dots, 25\}</m>, and let <m>n</m> be a positive integer. Define 
    <md>
        \mathcal{K}=\mathcal{P}=\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{A}^n
    </md>, 
    and encryption methods <m>E</m> and <m>D</m> as <md>
        <mrow>E_{(k_1,k_2,\dots, k_n)}(p_1,p_2,\dots, p_n) \amp = (p_1+k_1\boldmod{26}, p_2+k_2\boldmod{26},\dots p_n+k_n\boldmod{26})</mrow>
        <mrow>D_{(k_1,k_2,\dots, k_n)}(c_1,c_2,\dots, c_n) \amp = (c_1-k_1\boldmod{26}, c_2-k_2\boldmod{26},\dots c_n-k_n\boldmod{26})</mrow>
    </md>
    for each key <m>k=(k_1,k_2,\dots, k_n)\in \mathcal{K}</m>, plaintext <m>p=(p_1,p_2,\dots, p_n)\in \mathcal{P}</m> and ciphertext <m>c=(c_1,c_2,\dots, c_n)\in \mathcal{C}</m>.
    </p>
    </statement>
    </definition>
<project xml:id="sage_vignere">
        <title>Vigenère cipher: encryption and decryption</title>
        <p>
           Again, using Sage we can easily implement the Vigenère cipher. 
        </p>
        <sage>
            <input>
                AlphaNum={}
for x in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz':
    AlphaNum[x]=ord(x)-97
NumAlpha={y:x for (x,y) in AlphaNum.items()}

def vig_enc(m,k):
    r=len(k)
    seq=[]
    for x in k:
        seq.append(AlphaNum[x])
    c=''
    for i in range(len(m)):
        c=c+NumAlpha[(AlphaNum[m[i]]+seq[i%r])%26]
    return c
def vig_dec(c,k):
    kbar=''
    for x in k:
        kbar=kbar+NumAlpha[-AlphaNum[x]%26]
    return vig_enc(c,kbar)
            </input>
            <output>
                
            </output>
        </sage>
        <sage>
            <input>
                c=''
            </input>
            <output>
                
            </output>
        </sage>  
        <sage>
            <input>
                
            </input>
            <output>
                
            </output>
        </sage> 
    </project>      

</subsection>

</section>